---
// §4.2.2 SiteHeader — global header. Sticky is OFF (static, scrolls away), 1px bottom
// border, background --color-bg (not surface). NO Stats link in the header: Stats lives
// in the footer only (§4.2.4, §9.9).
//
// ── F1/F2 (operator, Stage 5B) — SUPERSEDES §4.5's "no JS, no hamburger" nav collapse.
// The wrap-everything header stacked into THREE rows on a phone and ate the fold; the
// operator's verdict was "much less busy". So: in the narrow mode the whole menu lives in a
// slide-in drawer behind one `menu` toggle, and in the wide mode the full inline row shows.
//
// ══ THE FIT BUDGET (operator, 2026-08-30) — read this before touching the nav ═══════════
//
// THE HEADER IS NOT AS WIDE AS THE VIEWPORT. It lives inside the sponsor-rails "dissolving
// wrapper": from the DERIVED rail breakpoint (src/lib/sponsorLayout.ts — CONTENT_MIN 900 +
// SIDE_COST 376 = 1276px) the rails narrow `--container-max` to make their own gutters, and
// `.container-page` narrows with it. So the header's usable content box is NOT `100vw`:
//
//     vw <  1276 : content box = min(1200, vw) − 2×24 page gutter
//     vw >= 1276 : content box = 0.6·vw + 134.4 − 48       (rail arithmetic, measured)
//
// which BOTTOMS OUT at CONTENT_MIN − 48 = **852px**, at vw ≈ 1276–1280 — i.e. at exactly the
// laptop widths the rails exist to serve. Measured on the built page at 1280: 854px.
//
// That is the number every header decision has to answer to, and the previous cut did not:
// the inline row's intrinsic width was 1015px (brand 154 + nav 432 + right cluster 365 +
// 2 × 32px group gutters), so at 1280 and 1440 the nav was squeezed and WRAPPED to a second
// row, and a 1024–1078 sliver wrapped from the page gutters alone. The boundary being `lg`
// (1024) was never the bug — a viewport number cannot see a container that is narrowed
// underneath it.
//
// THREE CHANGES, in the order they matter:
//
//   1. SHORTER LABEL. `shareable bots` → `bots` (CP-148). −80px. The hub is still called
//      Shareable Bots everywhere it is introduced; only its chrome label shrank.
//
//   2. A `more ▾` DROPDOWN for the utility links. The PRIMARY content nav stays inline and
//      visible — bots · use cases · collections · wall · news — and the two utility doors,
//      `/agent` and `submit`, move into a compact CSS-only disclosure in the right cluster.
//      −60px, and it is the place the NEXT utility link goes for free.
//
//   3. TIGHTER GUTTERS. Group gutter 32 → 24, in-group gutter 24 → 20. −32px. The hierarchy
//      (groups further apart than the items inside them) survives; the roomy 32/24 pair was
//      sized for a header that had 1152px to spend, which it only ever has above 1776px.
//
//   Together, measured on the built page at 1280: 1015 → **808px** (brand 154 + nav 336 +
//   cluster 270 + 2 × 24px group gutters) against an 852px floor — 46px of slack at the
//   tightest width the site can ever hand this header. `connect` remains the ONE amber
//   element (§A1) and it stays last, in the position of prominence.
//
// ══ THE GUARANTEE — a CONTAINER query, not a viewport query ═════════════════════════════
//
// Because the header's width is not the viewport's, the inline-row-vs-drawer decision is
// driven off the header's OWN container box: `.siteHeader__bar` is `container-type:
// inline-size` and the inline row is switched on by `@container siteheader (min-width:
// INLINE_MIN)`. The row is shown only where it genuinely fits, and everywhere else — a
// phone, a tablet, an extreme squeeze, or a future sixth nav link — it falls back to the
// drawer instead of wrapping. There is no `lg` breakpoint in this file any more, and no
// number here that has to be kept in sync with the rails.
//
//   ► INLINE_MIN is 840px = the measured 808 plus 32px of tolerance (a mono-fallback flash
//     while the webfont loads renders the nav a few percent wider). It appears in exactly
//     TWO places, adjacent, both marked: the `@container` condition and the
//     `--header-inline-min` custom property the island reads. IF YOU ADD A NAV LINK:
//     re-measure the row's intrinsic width at 1280 and raise both. While INLINE_MIN stays
//     under 852 the inline row is guaranteed across the ENTIRE rails band; push it over and
//     the site simply serves the drawer at 1276–1300 — degraded, never broken, never wrapped.
//
//   ► CONSEQUENCE, and it is the point rather than a side effect: the collapse point is no
//     longer 1024. The row now needs 808px, so it fits from a ~888px viewport, and that is
//     where the drawer takes over. The old `lg` boundary was never a design choice — the
//     previous cut's own note says "those eight items plus their gutters do not fit one row
//     until ~1024px". They fit at 888 now, so that is the line. Note also that you CANNOT
//     have both "collapse at 1024" and "inline row inside the rails" from one threshold:
//     1024 implies a 976px requirement, which is wider than the 852px the rails leave. That
//     contradiction is exactly why this decision moved off the viewport axis.
//
// The drawer is the deliberate home for a growing nav: its panel is `overflow-y: auto` with
// `overscroll-behavior: contain`, so link six, ten and twenty cost nothing but scroll — the
// inline row is the fragile surface, the drawer is the elastic one. ONE `nav` array feeds
// both surfaces, so a link cannot arrive in one and not the other.
//
// Baseline note: container queries and `:has()` are both below Tailwind v4's own floor
// (Safari 16.4 / Chrome 111 / Firefox 128), so this adds no browser requirement the
// stylesheet did not already impose. There is no viewport-media fallback ON PURPOSE — a
// second mechanism could disagree with the first and show two menus or none.
//
// The drawer is a native `<details>`: it opens, closes and is keyboard-operable with ZERO
// JS. The island below is ENHANCEMENT ONLY (scroll lock, focus trap, scrim tap, Escape, and
// the same four-way close for the `more` dropdown), exactly the shape §4.2.9 already
// sanctions for FilterBar, so §4.4 rule 7 holds — no JS-dependent interaction is
// load-bearing. Zero inline JS: §10.7's CSP is `script-src 'self'`.
//
// ── Surface 1 of the "connect to THE FEED" system (operator, 2026-08-22) ────────────────
// The header carries the persistent entry point to `#install-site` — the ONE feed panel
// BaseLayout renders sitewide. It is a real anchor with `data-install-trigger`, so it opens
// the panel through the island with JS and through `:target` without it.
//
// §A1 ACCENT SWAP, not an accent ADDITION. The header's one amber element used to be
// `submit`; it is now `connect your bot`, and `submit` drops to the muted NavLink treatment
// the /agent door already uses. Net amber count per viewport is unchanged at exactly one, in
// all three header contexts (inline cluster · narrow bar · drawer). If you are tempted to
// give `submit` its fill back, that is a §A1 decision, not a styling one.
import MarkGlyph from './MarkGlyph.astro';
import NavLink from './NavLink.astro';
import Button from './Button.astro';
import ThemeToggle from './ThemeToggle.astro';
import { CP_126_CONNECT_HEADER, CP_127_CONNECT_SHORT, CP_148_MARKETPLACE_NAV } from '../lib/copy';

interface Props {
  currentPath: string;
}

const { currentPath } = Astro.props;
const isCurrent = (href: string) =>
  href === '/' ? currentPath === '/' : currentPath.startsWith(href);

// SEO note (BUILD-NOTES): every hub keeps at least one internal link path — entry
// breadcrumbs link `/plugins/`, `/use-cases/` and `/collections/` (F14), the footer's browse
// column links all of them plus `/categories/` and `/integrations/`, and the home table's
// category chips link the category hubs. Nothing is orphaned.
//
// PRIMARY nav — content destinations. One array feeds BOTH the inline row and the drawer, so
// a link cannot arrive in one and not the other. Adding to this array costs ~95px of the
// inline row's budget; see THE FIT BUDGET above before you do.
const nav = [
  { href: '/marketplace/', label: CP_148_MARKETPLACE_NAV },
  { href: '/use-cases/', label: 'use cases' },
  { href: '/collections/', label: 'collections' },
  { href: '/wall/', label: 'wall' },
  { href: '/news/', label: 'news' },
];

// UTILITY nav — doors, not destinations. Inline these live behind `more ▾`; in the drawer
// they sit below the rule. This is where the next utility link goes: the dropdown grows
// downwards for free, the inline row does not grow sideways for free.
const utility = [
  { href: '/agent/', label: '/agent', glyph: true },
  { href: '/submit/', label: 'submit', glyph: false },
];
---

<header class="siteHeader border-b border-border bg-bg" data-pagefind-ignore>
  {/* THE QUERY CONTAINER. `.container-page` gives it the (rail-narrowed) max-width and the
      page gutters; `container-type: inline-size` below makes its CONTENT BOX the thing every
      mode decision in this file is asked about. It carries no flex of its own — the row
      inside does — because a container query may not style its own container, and the row's
      gap/height/padding are part of the mode. */}
  <div class="siteHeader__bar container-page">
    {/* `gap-x-2` in the narrow mode, `gap-x-6` inline (set in the container query). The
        narrow bar carries FOUR controls (brand · theme · connect · menu) and the numbers are
        genuinely tight: measured at 390px the four intrinsic widths total 315px against
        358px of content box, so a 32px gutter between them wrapped `menu` onto a second row
        — the exact three-row failure F1 was written to kill. 8px holds the row at 360px and
        up. See `.headerConnect` and the narrow-phone block below for the other two thirds of
        the fit. `flex-wrap` stays ON as the F6 safety valve: if anything ever did overflow,
        a second header row is a far smaller failure than a page that scrolls sideways. */}
    <div class="siteHeader__row flex min-h-12 flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-4 py-2">
      {/* `flex-1` in the narrow mode, where it is what pushes the drawer toggle to the right
          edge. Inline it must shrink to its content or it steals width from the nav and
          forces a second row — measured at F1 QA. Both states are set in the scoped CSS so
          the mode lives in ONE place. */}
      <a href="/" class="brand flex items-center gap-3" aria-label="grokbot.dev home">
        {/* Direct round: mark is INK (grid) + the amber accent dot from grid() itself.
            `text-accent` here painted the WHOLE grid amber — part of "logo icon is off". */}
        <MarkGlyph size={30} class="brand-mark text-text" />
        <span class="brand-word display-track font-display font-semibold lowercase text-text">
          grokbot.dev
        </span>
      </a>

      {/* `flex-wrap: nowrap` is load-bearing, not decoration: the OLD failure was not the bar
          wrapping, it was this nav being handed less width than it needed (it was `flex-1`,
          so it shrank) and wrapping its own links onto a second line. It no longer shrinks
          and no longer wraps — if it does not fit, the container query hands the whole menu
          to the drawer instead. */}
      <nav class="siteHeader__nav" aria-label="main">
        {nav.map((item) => <NavLink href={item.href} label={item.label} current={isCurrent(item.href)} />)}
      </nav>

      <div class="siteHeader__cluster">
        {/* ── `more ▾` — the compact utility dropdown (operator, 2026-08-30) ───────────────
            A native `<details>`, like the drawer: it opens, closes and is keyboard-operable
            with ZERO JS and ZERO inline script, so it is CSP-clean by construction. The
            island only adds what markup cannot do — click-outside, Escape, focus-out and
            close-on-navigate. It is styled as a muted nav item rather than a bordered
            control on purpose: it IS nav, and a second bordered control beside the theme
            toggle would have cost another ~18px of a budget that has none to give. */}
        <details class="navMore" data-nav-more>
          <summary class="navMore__toggle" aria-label="more links">
            <span>more</span><span class="navMore__caret" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="navMore__panel" role="group" aria-label="more links">
            {utility.map((item) => (
              <a
                href={item.href}
                class:list={['navMore__link', isCurrent(item.href) && 'navMore__link--current']}
                aria-current={isCurrent(item.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
              >
                {item.glyph && <span aria-hidden="true" class="agent-glyph text-accent">▪</span>}
                {item.label}
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </details>

        {/* F9 — sits with the other chrome, left of the one amber element, which keeps
            A1's "one accent per viewport" position of prominence at the end of the row. */}
        <ThemeToggle variant="inline" />

        <Button
          href="#install-site"
          variant="solid"
          size="sm"
          label={CP_126_CONNECT_HEADER}
          attrs={{ 'data-install-trigger': 'install-site' }}
        />
      </div>

      {/* ── the slide-in drawer (narrow mode). Native <details>; the island only enhances it.
          F7: spacing is expressed as Tailwind utilities, NOT as `var(--spacing-N)` in the
          scoped CSS. Tailwind v4 defines a single `--spacing` multiplier and computes
          `calc(var(--spacing) * n)` inside each utility — it never emits `--spacing-6`, so my
          first cut's `padding: var(--spacing-6)` resolved to nothing and the drawer shipped
          with no padding at all. That is exactly what the operator saw. Utilities can't
          silently evaluate to nothing. */}
      {/* Direct round: light/dark is VISIBLE in the drawer-band header now, not inside the
          drawer — moved, not duplicated. The wrapper (not the button) carries the display
          switch, because the button's scoped `.themeToggle{display:inline-flex}` outranks a
          utility and would keep it visible in the inline mode otherwise. It must disappear
          exactly where the inline cluster's own ThemeToggle appears, which is why BOTH are
          switched by the same container query and nothing else. */}
      <span class="siteHeader__themeMobile"><ThemeToggle variant="inline" /></span>

      {/* The narrow-mode CTA rides in the BAR, not only in the drawer: an action worth putting
          in every header is worth reaching without opening a menu first. It carries CP-127
          (`connect`) rather than CP-126 — measured at 360/375px, the long label is wider than
          the row can give it beside the brand, the theme toggle and `menu`, and F1's whole
          point was that this header stays ONE row on a phone. */}
      <a href="#install-site" data-install-trigger="install-site" class="headerConnect">
        {CP_127_CONNECT_SHORT}
      </a>

      <details class="navDrawer" data-nav-drawer>
        <summary class="navDrawer__toggle" aria-label="menu">
          <span class="navDrawer__word navDrawer__word--closed">menu</span>
          <span class="navDrawer__word navDrawer__word--open" aria-hidden="true">✕</span>
        </summary>

        {/* Tap-outside target. Enhancement only — without JS you close with the same control. */}
        <span class="navDrawer__scrim" data-nav-scrim aria-hidden="true"></span>

        <div
          class="navDrawer__panel flex flex-col gap-8 p-6 pt-20"
          data-nav-panel
          tabindex="-1"
          role="group"
          aria-label="site menu"
        >
          <nav class="navDrawer__nav flex flex-col gap-1" aria-label="mobile">
            {nav.map((item) => (
              <a
                href={item.href}
                class:list={[
                  'navDrawer__link',
                  isCurrent(item.href) && 'navDrawer__link--current',
                ]}
                aria-current={isCurrent(item.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
              >
                {item.label}
              </a>
            ))}

            <span class="navDrawer__rule my-3" aria-hidden="true"></span>

            {/* The SAME utility array the `more ▾` dropdown renders — the drawer can never be
                missing a door the inline row has. */}
            {utility.map((item) => (
              <a
                href={item.href}
                class:list={['navDrawer__link', isCurrent(item.href) && 'navDrawer__link--current']}
                aria-current={isCurrent(item.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
              >
                {item.glyph && <span aria-hidden="true" class="agent-glyph text-accent">▪</span>}
                {item.label}
              </a>
            ))}
          </nav>

          {/* A1 accent law: connect is the ONE amber element in the drawer. */}
          <a href="#install-site" data-install-trigger="install-site" class="navDrawer__cta">
            {CP_126_CONNECT_HEADER}
          </a>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<style>
  /* ══ THE MODE SWITCH — one container, one threshold, no viewport numbers ════════════════
     Everything that differs between the inline row and the narrow bar is decided HERE, by
     the header's own content-box width. See the frontmatter's FIT BUDGET block for why a
     viewport media query cannot do this job (the rails narrow the container underneath it).

     ► INLINE_MIN = 840px. It appears twice — the `@container` condition at the BOTTOM of
       this stylesheet and the custom property right here, which exists so the island can
       read the number instead of carrying a third copy. Measured requirement of the inline
       row is 808px (at 1280, fonts loaded); 840 carries 32px of webfont-swap tolerance; the
       tightest content box the site ever hands this header is 852px, at vw ≈ 1276-1280.
       Re-measure and raise BOTH if you add a nav link. */
  .siteHeader__bar {
    container-type: inline-size;
    container-name: siteheader;
    --header-inline-min: 840;
  }

  /* Belt and braces for the drawer's `position: fixed` panel. Chrome 145 was measured NOT to
     make a `container-type` element a containing block for fixed descendants (a synthetic
     `position:fixed; inset:0` probe stayed viewport-sized, where `contain: layout` shrank it
     to the bar) — but that is an implementation detail of one engine, and the drawer's panel
     / scrim / X depend on it in every engine. So: while the drawer is OPEN the container is
     switched off entirely. The `@container` rules below then stop matching, the base (narrow)
     mode applies, and the panel is viewport-positioned no matter how an engine reads the
     spec. This is safe by construction — the drawer can only be opened from the narrow mode,
     so "force narrow mode while it is open" changes nothing a user can see. */
  .siteHeader__bar:has([data-nav-drawer][open]) {
    container-type: normal;
  }

  /* ── NARROW MODE (the base — a phone, a tablet, any squeezed container) ───────────────
     The INLINE-mode half of this switch is the LAST block in this stylesheet, on purpose:
     `@container` adds no specificity, so it can only override these by coming after them.
     Do not move it up. */
  .brand {
    /* what pushes the drawer toggle to the right edge */
    flex: 1 1 auto;
  }
  /* Both groups are `nowrap` in every mode: the header may hand the whole menu to the
     drawer, but it may never wrap a group onto a second line. This is the half of the fix
     the OLD failure needed — the nav was `flex-1`, so it shrank below its intrinsic width
     and wrapped its own links. It no longer shrinks. 1.25rem = gap-x-5. */
  .siteHeader__nav,
  .siteHeader__cluster {
    display: none;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    align-items: center;
    column-gap: 1.25rem;
  }
  .siteHeader__themeMobile {
    display: inline-flex;
  }

  /* §4.2.2 — the machine door is marked with an 8px ▪ glyph. */
  .agent-glyph {
    font-size: 8px;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  /* F2 — the phone lockup was too small next to the nav type. `!important` is load-bearing
     here and nowhere else: §4.2.1 MarkGlyph writes its size as an INLINE style, which no
     class can outrank. Changing that documented prop API for one caller would be the bigger
     change. */
  /* `:global()` is required, not stylistic: the mark is rendered by the MarkGlyph CHILD
     component, so it never receives this component's scope attribute and a plain
     `.brand-mark` selector silently matches nothing. `.brand-word` is in this template and
     scopes normally. Caught at F2 QA — the step was a no-op without it. */
  /* Direct round — operator: "it needs to be much larger in the header." One size at every
     width now: 30px mark, --text-xl wordmark inline (narrow keeps --text-lg for fit). */
  :global(.brand-mark) {
    inline-size: 30px !important;
    block-size: 30px !important;
  }
  .brand-word {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
  }

  /* ---------- `more ▾` — the utility dropdown (inline mode only) ---------- */
  .navMore {
    position: relative;
  }
  /* Muted nav voice, not a bordered control: it is a nav item that reveals nav items, and
     the budget could not afford a second bordered box beside the theme toggle. */
  .navMore__toggle {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    /* the §4.6 24px target floor, without the padding a bordered control would need */
    min-block-size: 1.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-transform: lowercase;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    transition: color 0.15s;
  }
  .navMore__toggle::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }
  .navMore__toggle:hover,
  .navMore[open] .navMore__toggle {
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
  .navMore__caret {
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .navMore__caret {
      transition: transform 0.15s;
    }
  }
  .navMore[open] .navMore__caret {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  /* Anchored to the toggle, not to the bar: `.navMore` is the positioned ancestor, so this
     is unaffected by whether the query container is a containing block in any given engine.
     z-index 30 clears the sticky section headers (20) and the rails (20) it opens over, and
     stays under the install modal (60) and the drawer (40/41). */
  .navMore__panel {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: calc(100% + 0.75rem);
    inset-inline-end: 0;
    z-index: 30;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-inline-size: 9rem;
    padding: 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    /* the one place the header floats over content — it needs to read as a layer */
    box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.12);
  }
  .navMore__link {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    /* §4.6 target size — the same 44px rows the drawer uses, minus its full-bleed width */
    min-block-size: 2.25rem;
    padding-inline: 0.5rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-transform: lowercase;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    transition: color 0.15s, background 0.15s;
  }
  .navMore__link:hover {
    color: var(--color-text);
    background: var(--color-bg);
  }
  .navMore__link--current {
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  /* ---------- the narrow-mode connect pill (Surface 1) ---------- */
  /* Sized to sit level with the `menu` toggle — same 36px block size, same radius, same mono
     — so the bar reads as two controls of one family rather than a CTA bolted onto a header.
     36px is the height the operator already settled on for `menu` (down from 44) and it
     clears SC 2.5.8's 24px floor with room; matching it beats re-litigating that decision on
     the button next to it. `margin-inline-start: auto` is deliberately NOT set here — the
     drawer carries it, so the pill sits immediately left of `menu` at the right edge.
     It is hidden in the inline mode, where the cluster owns the CTA with the long CP-126
     label; that hand-off is in the container query above, with everything else. */
  .headerConnect {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    min-block-size: 36px;
    /* --text-xs + 8px of inline padding is the smallest this can be and still read as a
       button. It is the §4.2.7 `sm` button's type size; the padding is tighter than that
       spec's 12px because the bar has 43px of slack at 390 and none at 360. */
    padding-inline: 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--color-accent-strong);
    color: var(--color-accent-contrast);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    text-transform: lowercase;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .headerConnect:hover {
    filter: brightness(0.9);
  }
  .headerConnect:active {
    transform: translateY(1px);
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .headerConnect:active {
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---------- narrow-phone fit (≤389px: 360, 375, 384 …) ----------
     The last 8px. Below 390 the four controls plus their gutters run past the content box
     and `menu` drops to a second row, so the LOCKUP gives back what the new control takes:
     the mark stays at its full 30px (the operator's "much larger in the header" was about
     the mark, and it is untouched) and only the wordmark steps --text-lg → --text-base with
     a tighter mark/word gap. Measured 360px: 127 + 50 + 68 + 55 + 24 = 324 against 328.
     This is a FIT rule with a reason, not a taste rule — if the bar ever loses a control,
     delete it rather than keeping a smaller wordmark for no cause.
     A VIEWPORT query is correct here and a container query is not: below 390 the container
     IS the viewport minus the gutters, and this rule is about the phone, not about a box. */
  @media (max-width: 389px) {
    .brand {
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }
    .brand-word {
      font-size: var(--text-base);
    }

    /* ── THE DARK-MODE 8px (found at the 2026-08-30 QA sweep, PRE-DATING it) ─────────────
       The measurement above was taken in LIGHT, and the theme toggle names the mode you
       would switch TO — so it reads `dark` (4 chars) in light and `light` (5 chars) in dark.
       One character of Geist Mono at --text-sm is ~8px, and the 360px bar had exactly ZERO
       slack (304 + 3×8 = 328 against a 328px content box), so in DARK the bar was 8px over
       and `menu` dropped to a second row. Verified on live production at 360px with
       `gb-theme=dark`: header 105px tall, toggle at y=60. It was never a light-mode bug,
       which is why five rounds of QA never saw it.
       The 8px comes back out of the two chrome controls' horizontal padding rather than out
       of the gutters (8px between four controls is already the documented floor) or the
       lockup (the operator's "much larger in the header"). Both keep their 36px block size,
       so §4.6 / SC 2.5.8 target sizes are untouched — only the side padding moves.
       `:global()` is required for the toggle: it is rendered by the ThemeToggle CHILD
       component and never carries this file's scope attribute (same trap as `.brand-mark`). */
    .siteHeader__themeMobile :global(.themeToggle) {
      padding-inline: 0.25rem;
    }
    .navDrawer__toggle {
      padding-inline: 0.5rem;
    }
  }
  /* ---------- sub-360 phones (320, 344 …) ----------
     Below 360 the content box falls under the 328px floor measured above and `menu` wraps to
     a second row again. Every control is already at its documented floor — the gutters are at
     8px, and both chrome controls keep their 36px block size for SC 2.5.8 — so the only
     element left that can give back real width is the WORDMARK, and it is worth ~68px.
     The 30px mark stays (the operator's "much larger in the header" was always about the
     mark), and the link keeps `aria-label="grokbot.dev home"`, so the home affordance and its
     accessible name both survive the word being hidden. `display: none` and not a font step:
     at 320px a smaller word still would not fit, and half-measures here cost another QA round.
     360-389px is deliberately untouched — it MEASURES as fitting, so it keeps its wordmark. */
  @media (max-width: 359px) {
    .brand-word {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---------- drawer (F1, polished at F7) ---------- */
  .navDrawer {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
  }

  /* The ONE control: `menu` in the header when closed, `✕` inside the panel when open.
     Keeping it as the <summary> means the drawer still opens and closes with zero JS — a
     second, JS-only close button would be a control that silently does nothing. */
  .navDrawer__toggle {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Direct round: slimmed from 44px — the header was spending too much vertical
       space on it. 36px still clears SC 2.5.8's 24px floor comfortably. */
    min-inline-size: 40px;
    min-block-size: 36px;
    padding-inline: 0.65rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-interactive);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-transform: lowercase;
    color: var(--color-text);
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .navDrawer__toggle::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  /* F7 — while open it becomes the X, pinned inside the panel's top-right padding box.
     `fixed` (not `relative`) so it lands at a known inset rather than wherever the header
     happens to have placed it; z-index 42 keeps it above the panel, which is what F1 QA
     caught it failing to do. */
  .navDrawer[open] .navDrawer__toggle {
    position: fixed;
    inset-block-start: 1.5rem;
    inset-inline-end: 1.5rem;
    z-index: 42;
    inline-size: 44px;
    padding-inline: 0;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
  }
  .navDrawer__word--open,
  .navDrawer[open] .navDrawer__word--closed {
    display: none;
  }
  .navDrawer[open] .navDrawer__word--open {
    display: inline;
  }

  .navDrawer__scrim {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 40;
    display: block;
    background: var(--color-text);
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  .navDrawer__panel {
    position: fixed;
    inset-block: 0;
    inset-inline-end: 0;
    z-index: 41;
    /* C4: ~80% width, capped so it never becomes a full-bleed sheet on a large phone. */
    inline-size: 80%;
    max-inline-size: 22rem;
    /* internally scrollable — a long nav must never be unreachable */
    overflow-y: auto;
    overscroll-behavior: contain;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    /* C4 custom scrollbar — thin, in our own tokens, not the platform default. */
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    scrollbar-color: var(--color-border-strong) transparent;
  }
  .navDrawer__panel::-webkit-scrollbar {
    inline-size: 6px;
  }
  .navDrawer__panel::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
    background: var(--color-border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }
  .navDrawer__panel::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
    background: transparent;
  }

  /* F7 slide-in. The transform/opacity start state is applied ONLY when the island has
     marked the drawer with `data-nav-animate`, so with JS off the panel simply appears in
     place instead of being stranded off-screen at translateX(100%) forever. Transform and
     opacity only — no layout is animated. */
  [data-nav-animate] .navDrawer__panel {
    transform: translateX(100%);
    transition: transform 280ms ease-out;
  }
  [data-nav-animate] .navDrawer__scrim {
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 280ms ease-out;
  }
  [data-nav-animate] .navDrawer__toggle {
    transition: opacity 280ms ease-out;
  }
  [data-nav-animate].is-open .navDrawer__panel {
    transform: translateX(0);
  }
  [data-nav-animate].is-open .navDrawer__scrim {
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  /* §4.4 rule 6 — reduced motion gets the end state instantly, never a slide. The global
     rule already crushes durations; this makes the intent local and explicit. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    [data-nav-animate] .navDrawer__panel,
    [data-nav-animate] .navDrawer__scrim,
    [data-nav-animate] .navDrawer__toggle {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .navDrawer__link {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    min-block-size: 44px;
    padding-block: 0.25rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    text-transform: lowercase;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    transition: color 0.15s;
  }
  .navDrawer__link:hover {
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
  .navDrawer__link--current {
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }
  .navDrawer__rule {
    display: block;
    block-size: 1px;
    background: var(--color-border);
  }

  .navDrawer__cta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-block-size: 44px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--color-accent-strong);
    color: var(--color-accent-contrast);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-transform: lowercase;
  }
  .navDrawer__cta:hover {
    filter: brightness(0.9);
  }

  /* ══ INLINE MODE — the other half of the mode switch declared at the top ════════════════
     LAST IN THE FILE ON PURPOSE. A container query adds no specificity of its own, so every
     property it flips (`display` on the two groups, the mobile theme, `.headerConnect` and
     `.navDrawer`; `flex` and `font-size` on the brand; the row's gap/height/padding) can
     only be overridden from here by SOURCE ORDER. Move this block up and the base rules
     below it silently win — the nav disappears on desktop and nothing errors.
     The one rule allowed to come after it is the ≤389px narrow-phone block above, which is
     unreachable from here: a 389px viewport is a 357px container, and this needs 840. */
  @container siteheader (min-width: 840px) {
    /* The roomy row: 4rem = min-h-16, 1rem = py-4, 1.5rem = gap-x-6 on the Tailwind scale.
       Expressed here rather than as `lg:` utilities because the mode is ONE decision and it
       lives in ONE place; scoped CSS is unlayered, so it outranks the utilities on the row. */
    .siteHeader__row {
      min-block-size: 4rem;
      column-gap: 1.5rem;
      padding-block: 1rem;
    }
    .brand {
      /* must shrink to its content here or it steals width from the nav */
      flex: 0 0 auto;
    }
    .brand-word {
      font-size: var(--text-xl);
    }
    .siteHeader__nav {
      display: flex;
    }
    .siteHeader__cluster {
      display: flex;
      /* the leftover width sits here, so the cluster is pinned to the right edge and no
         group is ever asked to shrink */
      margin-inline-start: auto;
    }
    .siteHeader__themeMobile,
    .headerConnect,
    .navDrawer {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // SANCTIONED ISLAND (operator directive, Stage 5B F1) — the mobile nav drawer, plus the
  // `more ▾` dropdown added 2026-08-30. ENHANCEMENT ONLY, in the shape §4.2.9 already
  // sanctions for FilterBar: both are native <details> that open, close and are
  // keyboard-operable with no JS at all. This adds the things markup cannot do — background
  // scroll lock, focus trap, scrim tap, Escape-to-close, and click-outside for the dropdown.
  // With JS off, everything still works via `menu` / `more`.
  const drawer = document.querySelector<HTMLDetailsElement>('[data-nav-drawer]');
  if (drawer) {
    const panel = drawer.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-nav-panel]');
    const scrim = drawer.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-nav-scrim]');
    const toggle = drawer.querySelector<HTMLElement>('summary');

    // F7 — opt the CSS into the slide only now that JS is running. Without this attribute
    // the panel has no transform, so a no-JS visitor gets it in place rather than parked
    // off-screen at translateX(100%).
    drawer.dataset.navAnimate = '';

    const SLIDE_MS = 280;
    const reduced = window.matchMedia?.('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

    const FOCUSABLE = 'a[href], button:not([disabled]), input:not([disabled])';

    // A <details> hides its content the instant `open` flips, so a close transition would
    // never be seen. Hold `open` true, play the slide-out, then drop it.
    let closing = 0;
    const close = (returnFocus: boolean) => {
      if (!drawer.open || closing) return;
      drawer.classList.remove('is-open');
      const finish = () => {
        window.clearTimeout(closing);
        closing = 0;
        drawer.open = false;
        document.documentElement.style.overflow = '';
        if (returnFocus) toggle?.focus();
      };
      if (reduced?.matches) return finish();
      closing = window.setTimeout(finish, SLIDE_MS);
    };

    // Intercept the summary's own click for the CLOSE direction only; opening stays native.
    toggle?.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      if (!drawer.open) return;
      event.preventDefault();
      close(true);
    });

    drawer.addEventListener('toggle', () => {
      if (!drawer.open) return;
      // Scroll lock on the root element, so the page behind cannot move while the drawer
      // is open. Paired with `overscroll-behavior: contain` on the panel.
      document.documentElement.style.overflow = 'hidden';
      // Next frame, so the browser has painted the off-screen start state and the
      // transition to translateX(0) actually runs instead of being coalesced away.
      requestAnimationFrame(() => drawer.classList.add('is-open'));
      // Focus the PANEL, not the first link. Focus must enter the drawer for the trap and
      // for screen readers, but landing it on a link paints the §4.6 focus ring around a
      // full-width row that reads like a text input and collides with the X. A container
      // with tabindex="-1" is the standard dialog answer: focus moves, nothing is styled.
      panel?.focus({ preventScroll: true });
    });

    scrim?.addEventListener('click', () => close(true));

    // The drawer's amber CTA opens `#install-site`, which is a FIXED overlay at z-index 60 —
    // it would otherwise render on top of a drawer that is still open behind it, with the
    // drawer's scroll lock still applied underneath the modal's. Close the drawer on the way
    // through. `returnFocus: false` matters: the modal has already moved focus into its own
    // sheet by then, and yanking it back to a link that is about to be display:none would
    // break the trap. The drawer's lock is an inline style and the modal's is a class, so
    // clearing one cannot clear the other.
    panel?.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-install-trigger]').forEach((trigger) => {
      trigger.addEventListener('click', () => close(false));
    });

    document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
      if (!drawer.open) return;

      if (event.key === 'Escape') {
        event.preventDefault();
        close(true);
        return;
      }

      if (event.key !== 'Tab' || !panel) return;

      // Focus trap: the toggle plus everything in the panel form the loop, so Shift+Tab off
      // the first item lands on `close` rather than escaping to the page behind.
      const items = [toggle, ...Array.from(panel.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(FOCUSABLE))].filter(
        (el): el is HTMLElement => Boolean(el)
      );
      if (!items.length) return;

      const first = items[0];
      const last = items[items.length - 1];
      const active = document.activeElement;

      if (event.shiftKey && active === first) {
        event.preventDefault();
        last.focus();
      } else if (!event.shiftKey && active === last) {
        event.preventDefault();
        first.focus();
      } else if (active instanceof HTMLElement && !items.includes(active)) {
        event.preventDefault();
        first.focus();
      }
    });

    // Widening past the fit threshold must not strand a locked scroll behind a hidden drawer.
    // THIS NO LONGER CARRIES A BREAKPOINT. The old code compared `innerWidth` to a hardcoded
    // 1024 that had to be kept in lockstep with the CSS, and the header's width is not the
    // viewport's anyway (the rails narrow it). It now asks the header's OWN container the
    // same question the container query asks — content-box width vs `--header-inline-min`,
    // read from the CSS so there is no second copy of the number. If they could ever
    // disagree, this is the one that yields: the CSS decides what is visible.
    const bar = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.siteHeader__bar');
    const fitsInline = () => {
      if (!bar) return false;
      const styles = getComputedStyle(bar);
      const min = Number.parseFloat(styles.getPropertyValue('--header-inline-min'));
      if (!Number.isFinite(min)) return false;
      const content =
        bar.getBoundingClientRect().width -
        Number.parseFloat(styles.paddingInlineStart) -
        Number.parseFloat(styles.paddingInlineEnd);
      return content >= min;
    };
    window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
      if (drawer.open && fitsInline()) close(false);
    });
  }

  // ── `more ▾` — the utility dropdown. Native <details> does open/close/keyboard on its own;
  // this adds the three things a disclosure used as a MENU needs and markup cannot give it:
  // close on click-outside, close on Escape (focus returned to the toggle), and close when
  // focus leaves entirely. Without JS it is still a working disclosure — you close it with
  // the same control you opened it with.
  const more = document.querySelector<HTMLDetailsElement>('[data-nav-more]');
  if (more) {
    const moreToggle = more.querySelector<HTMLElement>('summary');
    const closeMore = (returnFocus: boolean) => {
      if (!more.open) return;
      more.open = false;
      if (returnFocus) moreToggle?.focus();
    };

    document.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      if (!more.open) return;
      const target = event.target;
      if (target instanceof Node && more.contains(target)) return;
      closeMore(false);
    });

    document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
      if (event.key === 'Escape' && more.open) {
        event.preventDefault();
        closeMore(true);
      }
    });

    // Tabbing off the last link closes it, so a keyboard user is never left with an open
    // panel floating behind the rest of the page.
    more.addEventListener('focusout', (event) => {
      const next = event.relatedTarget;
      if (next instanceof Node && more.contains(next)) return;
      closeMore(false);
    });
  }
</script>
